    "created_at": "2016-09-07T20:05:37Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-09-07T20:05:37Z",
    "body": "OK.  Gecko was about to ship allow-popups-to-escape-sandbox when we discovered that in our implementation it breaks websites that do things like this:\n\n```\n<iframe srcdoc=\"<a href='http://mozilla.org' target='_blank'>Link to mozilla.org</a>\"\n        sandbox=\"allow-popups allow-popups-to-escape-sandbox allow-same-origin\n                 allow-scripts\">\n</iframe>\n```\n\nPer spec as currently written, https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/semantics.html#following-hyperlinks-2 step 3 lands in https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/browsers.html#the-rules-for-choosing-a-browsing-context-given-a-browsing-context-name which in step 5 will create a new browsing context but NOT set a \"one permitted sandboxed navigator\" on it, because the \"sandbox propagates to auxiliary browsing contexts flag\" is not set.\n\nThen https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/semantics.html#following-hyperlinks-2 step 9 lands in https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/browsers.html#navigate which in step 2 goes to https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/browsers.html#allowed-to-navigate and falls into case 3, which aborts negatively.  So the navigation is aborted.\n\nUnfortunately, we're being silly and actually following the spec for targeted `<a>` loads (but not for window.open; see https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=960545).\n\nAnyway, it's not clear to me what Chrome is doing here exactly, whether it ever enforces any sandboxing restrictions on navigation, or whether it's special-casing allow-popups-to-escape-sandbox or what.  In the meantime, chances are we will simply not be able to ship allow-popups-to-escape-sandbox.\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
